# ATS Update — 2026-09-04 (overnight session) GAE(lambda) + checkpoint version stamping

## Context
First real multi-hundred-episode run since the `rl-core-fixes` branch fix
(the return-normalisation bug). Goal: check whether the fix holds over a
long run, not just the first few episodes.

## Run 1: `py -3.12 main.py --no-gui --episodes 300` (resumed from that
morning's 20-episode checkpoint)
Reward is real and moving — nothing like the old permanent -100/-128 floor —
but non-monotonic:

| episodes | avg reward |
|---|---|
| 1–30    | 47.2 |
| 31–60   | 16.6 |
| 61–90   | -17.8 |
| 91–120  | 12.4 |
| 121–150 | 64.9 |
| 151–180 | 57.3 |
| 181–210 | 86.8 (peak) |
| 211–240 | 59.0 |
| 241–270 | 60.8 |
| 271–300 | 42.0 (declining) |

Climbs to a real peak, dips hard once, climbs again, then declines toward
the end. Critic loss is noisy (1–19 range) alongside near-zero actor loss —
consistent with a high-variance advantage signal, not a broken one.

## Diagnosis
`continual_learner.py`'s advantage was full n-step-to-go return minus the
(detached) current value — effectively Monte-Carlo advantage over up to a
256-step buffer. Unbiased, but high variance, which plausibly explains the
noisy non-monotonic trend above (this was on the known follow-up list from
the first fix).

## Change
- `config_rl.py`: added `GAE_LAMBDA = 0.95`.
- `mind/continual_learner.py`: `_do_ppo_update` now computes advantages via
  GAE(lambda) over the rollout-time (`old`) values stored per-transition,
  with a `done`-mask that zeroes both the bootstrap and the lambda-return
  propagation across episode boundaries. Critic target (`returns`) is now
  `advantage + old_value` instead of raw discounted-reward-to-go.
- Verified `sanity_train.py` still passes, and improved: final-100 mean
  reward +0.858 (was +0.34 pre-GAE), reach rate 84% (was 65%). Not a
  regression.
- **Checkpoint version stamping** (the follow-up flagged after the
  checkpoint-collapse incident earlier today): added
  `config_rl.REWARD_SCHEME_VERSION = 2`. `main.py` `_save_policy` stamps it;
  `_load_policy` now skips (does not silently resume) any checkpoint whose
  stamp doesn't match, logging why, and falls through to the next candidate
  or a fresh policy. This is exactly the failure class from
  `2026-09-04b ...`: an old checkpoint's critic calibrated under a different
  return/reward scheme, loaded silently, looking like a regression that
  wasn't one.
- Moved the pre-GAE 300-episode checkpoint (unstamped, and now genuinely
  stale relative to the new critic-target math) to
  `checkpoints/_pre_gae_300ep_validation_2026-09-04/` — filesystem only,
  `checkpoints/` is gitignored.
- Launched `py -3.12 main.py --no-gui --episodes 2000 --fresh` as the actual
  overnight run under `REWARD_SCHEME_VERSION=2` + GAE, to get a real read on
  whether reward trends up over a much longer horizon than 300 episodes.
  Result to be recorded in a follow-up entry once it completes.

